<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Ladakh</h1><p class="st-meta">930 CE – 1842 CE (approx.) · capital Leh</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Tibetan Buddhist kingdom high in the western Himalaya that lasted nine centuries, ruled from Leh by the Namgyal kings until the Dogra conquest.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the collapse of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/tibetan-empire">Tibetan Empire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyide-nyimagon">Nyimagon</a>, a descendant of the Tibetan royal line, founded a kingdom in western <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tibet-1912-1951">Tibet</a> around 930. His son received Maryul, the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a> valley around Leh, which became the core of Ladakh. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> revived there in the 11th century, when the translator <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rinchen-zangpo">Rinchen Zangpo</a> is credited with founding many temples.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The kingdom lived on trade between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tibet-1912-1951">Tibet</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, especially in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shawl">pashmina</a> wool, and on irrigated barley farming. Monasteries such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alchi-monastery">Alchi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hemis-monastery">Hemis</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thiksey-monastery">Thiksey</a> held great influence. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namgyal-dynasty">Namgyal dynasty</a> took power in the 15th century; its greatest king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sengge-namgyal">Sengge Namgyal</a>, built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leh-palace">Leh Palace</a> and patronised Hemis. After a war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-tingmosgang">Treaty of Tingmosgang</a> of 1684 fixed Ladakh's borders, with Mughal help on Ladakh's side.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Between 1834 and 1842 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dogras">Dogra</a> general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zorawar-singh">Zorawar Singh</a> conquered Ladakh for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulab-singh">Gulab Singh</a>, and it became part of the princely state of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/jammu-and-kashmir-princely-state">Jammu and Kashmir</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namgyal-dynasty">Namgyal</a> family kept the palace at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stok-palace">Stok</a>, and Ladakh is now a union territory of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-ladakh.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Ladakh" width="320" height="217"><figcaption>Image: A.H. Francke, S.S.Gergan and F.M. Hassnan · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_empire_of_King_Tsewang_Rnam_Rgyal_1.,_and_that_of_King_Jamyang_Rnam_Rgyal.,_about_1560_and_1600_A.D.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>930 CE – 1842 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Leh</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Nyimagon, Sengge Namgyal, Deldan Namgyal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Tibetan Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ladakhi, Classical Tibetan</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Maryul, Namgyal dynasty of Ladakh, Ladakh</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1386&amp;focus=kingdom-of-ladakh" aria-label="Kingdom of Ladakh on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6655 940 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Ladakh lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6975 1196L7